    {
      "id": "8dc4100f-7616-42e7-8303-a9b7e3e84716",
      "topic": "Sun",
      "source": "Space & Astronomy_wikipedia",
      "orginal_content": "The ancient Sumerians believed that the Sun was Utu, the god of justice and a helper-deity.Later, Utu was identified with the East Semitic god Shamash.From at least the Fourth Dynasty of Ancient Egypt, the Sun was worshipped as the god Ra, portrayed as a falcon-headed divinity surmounted by the solar disk.In the New Empire period, the Sun became identified with the dung beetle.In the form of the sun disc Aten, the Sun had a brief resurgence during the Amarna Period when it became the preeminent, if not only, divinity for the Pharaoh Akhenaten.The Egyptians portrayed Ra as being carried across the sky in a solar barque, accompanied by lesser gods.To the Greeks, he was Helios, carried by a chariot drawn by fiery horses.",
      "headers": [
        "Sumerian Beliefs About the Sun (Utu)",
        "Egyptian Worship of Ra",
        "Greek Representation of the Sun (Helios)",
        "Evolution of Solar Deities"
      ]
