      "id": "c853c41b-9045-4cf4-9095-582a7c7866d9",
      "topic": "Moon",
      "source": "Space & Astronomy_wikipedia",
      "orginal_content": "Orbital center The Earth and the Moon form the Earth\u2013Moon satellite system with a shared center of mass, or barycenter.This barycenter is 5,000 km (3,100 mi) (about a 3/4 of Earth's radius) from the Earth's center.This barycenter is slowly moving outward as tidal friction drains energy from the rotating pair, and eventually the barycenter will be outside the Earth.In a heliocentric frame of reference the Moon orbits the Sun, perturbed by Earth.This has led some scientists to argue that the Moon could be identified as a planet, both historically and qualitatively, adding that its mass would be enough to clear its orbit around the Sun if it were on its own.This would imply that the Earth\u2013Moon system is a double planet.The International Astronomical Union standards organization has no definition for planets in a binary system, or for what constitutes a double planet system, but most scientists agree that this would require the Moon-Earth barycenter to be outside of Earth.",
      "headers": [
