      "id": "c853c41b-9045-4cf4-9095-582a7c7866d9",
      "topic": "Moon",
      "source": "Space & Astronomy_wikipedia",
      "orginal_content": "Over time this tidal drag causes the Earth's rotation and the Moon's orbital period to match, resulting in the lighter body becoming tidally locked (as the Moon currently is).Theoretically, in 50 billion years, the Earth's rotation will slow to match the Moon's orbital period, causing the Earth to always present the same side to the Moon.Long before this would happen, however, the Sun will become a red giant, possibly engulfing the Earth and the Moon.If the Earth\u2013Moon system survives this engulfment, as a 2026 study suggests, the drag from the solar atmosphere could cause the orbit of the Moon to decay.Once the orbit of the Moon closes to a distance of 18,470 km (11,480 mi), it will cross Earth's Roche limit and the tidal interaction with Earth would break the Moon apart.It would turn into a ring system, with most of the orbiting rings decaying and falling to Earth.",
