      "id": "c853c41b-9045-4cf4-9095-582a7c7866d9",
      "topic": "Moon",
      "source": "Space & Astronomy_wikipedia",
      "orginal_content": "The moon's axial tilt with respect to the ecliptic is 1.5427\u00b0, so in each draconic year (346.62 days), the sun moves from being 1.5427\u00b0 north of the lunar equator to being 1.5427\u00b0 south of it and then back.This is analogous to how, on Earth, the sun's position moves from the Tropic of Cancer to the Tropic of Capricorn and back every tropical year.This puts the poles of the moon in darkness (or with only part of the sun visible) for half a draconic year and in sunlight for half a draconic year.The amount of sunlight falling on horizontal areas near the poles depends on the altitude angle of the sun.These \"seasons\" have little effect in more equatorial areas.",
