      "id": "c853c41b-9045-4cf4-9095-582a7c7866d9",
      "topic": "Moon",
      "source": "Space & Astronomy_wikipedia",
      "orginal_content": "Future Both the U.S. and Chinese lunar programs have the goal to establish a lunar base in the 2030s with their international partners, being the first crewed lunar landings since Apollo 17 in 1972.Following the Artemis III test flight in low Earth orbit scheduled for 2027, NASA plans to return humans to the lunar surface in 2028, with two Artemis IV astronauts landing on the lunar south pole.While the Apollo missions were explorational in nature, the Artemis program plans to establish a more permanent presence.To this end, the U.S. is partnering with industry for lunar communications, payload delivery, and in situ resource utilization through programs such as NASA's Commercial Lunar Payload Services, and DARPA lunar programs.China is continuing its ambitious Chang'e program, having announced joint missions with Russia's struggling Luna-Glob program.Two lunar survey missions are planned, Chang'e 7 in 2026 and Chang'e 8 in 2028, before a crewed landing in 2029 or 2030.Human interaction and presence The first extraterrestrial probes reached the Moon (Luna program) in 1959, just a year into the space age, after the first ever orbital flight.Since then, humans have sent a range of probes and people to the Moon.The first stay of people on the Moon was conducted in 1969, in a series of crewed exploration missions (the Apollo Program), the last having taken place in 1972.Uninterrupted presence has been the case through the remains of impactors, landings and lunar orbiters.Some landings and orbiters have maintained a small lunar infrastructure, providing continuous observation and communication at the Moon.Longterm missions continuing to be active are some orbiters such as the 2009-launched Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter surveilling the Moon for future missions, as well as some Landers such as the 2013-launched Chang'e 3 with its Lunar Ultraviolet Telescope still operational.Five retroreflectors have been installed on the Moon since the 1970s and since used for accurate measurements of the physical librations through laser ranging to the Moon.Increasing human activity in cislunar space as well as on the Moon's surface, particularly missions at the far side of the Moon or the lunar north and south polar regions, are in need for a lunar infrastructure.For that purpose, orbiters in orbits around the Moon or the Earth\u2013Moon Lagrange points, have since 2006 been operated.With highly eccentric orbits providing continuous communication, as with the orbit of Queqiao and Queqiao-2 relay satellite.",
