      "id": "62704f8e-4519-4eb7-9e70-3f4cd4ec8905",
      "topic": "Galaxy",
      "source": "Space & Astronomy_wikipedia",
      "orginal_content": "Within a billion years of a galaxy's formation, key structures begin to appear.Globular clusters, the central supermassive black hole, and a galactic bulge of metal-poor Population II stars form.The creation of a supermassive black hole appears to play a key role in actively regulating the growth of galaxies by limiting the total amount of additional matter added.During this early epoch, galaxies undergo a major burst of star formation.During the following two billion years, the accumulated matter settles into a galactic disc.A galaxy will continue to absorb infalling material from high-velocity clouds and dwarf galaxies throughout its life.",
      "headers": [
